@@@235d943359fca1570f3d0105792c71c35da55f82	lesskey: don't ignore HAVE_SNPRINTF	While defines.h is included at lesskey.h, and it does define
HAVE_SNPRINTF, till now it was ignored while building lesskey, which
resulted in snprintf being used even when HAVE_SNPRINTF is 0.

Now we have the SNPRINTF<N> macros, copied from less.h, and used
instead of snprintf in lesskey source files.

Note that lesstest also ignores HAVE_SNPRINTF, but it's not mandatory
so currently not handled.

TODO: maybe add lesscommon.h with unified handling of snprintf.

lesskey.h
lesskey_parse.c

@@@8de26dca02c2addf6a64b4e62737f972e37f5446	windows: mingw and old msvc: don't use _snprintf	Previously, msvc before 2015 and mingw defined snprintf as _snprintf.

This can be a problem because _snprintf is non-compliant and does not
null-terminate if the length exceeds the buffer.

Also, some non-latest mingw versions don't have _snprintf (gcc 10).

With mingw this was not needed at all, because mingw ensures there's
a compliant snprintf (both with msvcrt and with ucrt), so now mingw
uses the standard snprintf.

With msvc before 2015 there's indeed an issue, but now instead of
using the broken _snprintf we specify that we don't have snprintf,
so that the appropriate code paths are chosen (currently that uses
sprintf, but in the future there could be a better shim).

defines.wn

@@@fd458cd1d122cc8a0ba351b1db86107bb4648840	windows: mingw: Makefile.wng: support SHELL=sh (cross compiling)	Makefile.wng sets SHELL = cmd.exe, and previously used cmd.exe
commands (del, copy...) exclusively, so if it was invoked
with SHELL=sh then these commands failed.

Now, if SHELL is set to something else, then the normal posix commands
are used instead (cp, rm...).

This allows cross compilation to use Makefile.wng - which is a lot
simpler to use than the standard build procedure, e.g.:

make -f Makefile.wng CC=x86-64-w64-mingw32-gcc SHELL=sh [REGEX_PACKAGE=...]

Makefile.wng

@@@0e5bd366edd92d7a28e4a5b4d7b5ee549633a8f2	windows: mingw: Makefile.wng: use mkhelp.sh if perl is missing	On Windows perl can be installed, but it's a non-trivial dependency.

If perl is missing to process less.hlp, then try also the newly added
mkhelp.sh script (which uses od and awk in standard POSIX syntax).

The makefile already assumes grep and sed (for funcs.h), and it's
highly likely that sh, od, and awk are available as well.

The minimum requirements are now a mingw compiler, gnu make, sh or
cmd.exe, and gnu coreutils or busybox[-w32] - on *nix or Windows.

Makefile.wng
mkhelp.sh

@@@95fb231fb897114efdaa1f7cb76a67268e360a10	windows: mingw: Makefile.wng: support REGEX_PACKAGE=none	The C source code supported NO_REGEX since configurable regex was
introduced at 55b5b97, but it was not possible to use it because
in such case Makefile.wng still tried to enable some lib/objects
which might not be available.

Now REFEX_PACKAGE=none works and lets defines.wn set NO_REGEX=1.

This is likely useful only for testing, or when size really matters.

Makefile.wng

@@@fdaee1fe98ae920ed6186b3799e85568e5ee5bf0	windows: mingw: Makefile.wng: default to regcomp-local	Previously, the default REGEX_PACKAGE was posix, but this is not
available by default in most/all mingw-w64 setups (debian, fedora,
w64devkit, and more).

Additionally, the regex default prefix is /mingw-w64/mingw64/opt
(when using posix or gnu regex), which is not available in most
mingw setups (not MSYS2 mingw 64 bits, and not even cygwin), so
this mostly didn't work.

In contrast, regcomp-local works in any mingw setup and doesn't
require non-standard libs, so that's a safer default.

Also, the non-mingw default is regcomp-local, and that's also used
at the semi-official windows builds of less, so it's better to have
the same default on all windows builds.

Makefile.wng

@@@adfe1fb906b77becd6332ec305e20ab20877dc3d	windows: fix mouse-button-down encoding	Previously, pressing the left mouse button incorrectly reported that
X11MOUSE_BUTTON3 (right button) was pressed, and right or middle
buttons were incorrectly reported as modifiers (ctrl/shift/etc), as
they ended up outside the 2-bits which x11 mouse uses for button id.

Now left/middle/right are reported as x11 BUTTON 1/2/3, respectively.

This doesn't have any visible impact, because currently there's only
mouse-button-up binding, and less doesn't care which button it was.

However, the next commit will add right-mouse-button binding, so
ensure the windows mouse handling doesn't become broken.

screen.c

@@@0ffa65babc698a91f6d8d56c2a530c94226a86e6	mouse: bind right-click as jump to the "#" mark	Previously any mouse-button click marked the line with '#'.

Now left-click marks with '#', and right-click jumps to this mark.

In practice, and to support 2/3 buttons mice, we apply the jump
with any mouse button which is not X11MOUSE_BUTTON1 (left).

With the legacy x11 mouse report handler, a button-release event
doesn't have button ID, so to trigger on a specific button release,
a state was introduced which holds the last button-press.

