@@@a055278b98d406176ae4c21f92e019e1802687e0	windows: improve UTF-8 locale comment (no-op)	Commit ad440b6 added support for runtime UTF-8 locale, but incorrectly
documented that it's quite the same as a UTF-8 manifest, except
startup values which don't automatically become UTF-8 (like argv).

However, that's not quite true.

While the UTF-8 manifest affects all the A(NSI) Windows APIs, like
CreateFileA and fopen, the runtime setlocale method affects only
the libc/crt API, and so fopen is covered, but CreateFileA is not.

Fix the comment to mention that.

Currently "less" uses these non-libc ANSI Windows API:
- CreateFileA: only used with the ASCII name "CONIN$", so OK..
- FillConsoleOutputCharacterA: only used with spaces and other ASCII.
- ScrollConsoleScreenBufferA: same as above.
- WriteConsoleA: only used when the console CP is UTF8, so OK.
- {Get,Set}ConsoleTitleA: saves/restores the original title, and uses
  console CP regardless of manifest/locale. Will be fixed later.

main.c

@@@7342fd48f84cc10e976d1a68826ee2c249d56f4b	windows: fix console title with unicode file name	Since commit ad440b6, we can open unicode file names as UTF-8 if the
binary was built with UCRT, however, because this is a runtime
attempt, the build-time CP_ACP might be incorrect, and instead,
less_acp should be used to take into account successful UTF-8 locale,
as noted in a comment in that commit.

Do that for the console (window) title, which un-gibberish the text
with unicode file names.

command.c

@@@d7399711ce1d4bfd23ad4961d4c69fdfd4cbc3a8	windows: restore unicode console title correctly	"less" saves the console title on startup, and restores it on exit,
however, it does so using the A(NSI) win API, and so if the title
was originally unicode, then restoration may fail, depending on the
console and ANSI codepages and other factors.

Now the save and restore are with the W(ide) win API, so it doesn't
depend on any codepages.

This is unrelated to unicode file names, and works even when "less"
doesn't support unicode file names.

