* lessecho.nro.VER:
* lesskey.nro.VER: Do it.

less.nro.VER
lessecho.nro.VER
lesskey.nro.VER

@@@ddb22541807dd2c4c31fe5fb5b1ef8fd5f2e7435	Prevent URLs from oversetting line if possible.	AT&T troff had no means of designating break points in words that should
not be hyphenated; the program was developed in an era when file names
were 14 characters long at most and URLs were over a decade away.

groff, Heirloom Doctools troff, and mandoc support the `\:` escape
sequence, which solves the problem.

Adapt a technique from ncurses[1], using a string to access this escape
sequence only if the formatter claims groff compatibility.

URLs continue to be at risk of oversetting on other formatters.

Also sync the bug-reporting advice in lessecho(1) and lesskey(1) with
that of less(1).

Further, I would not count on any formatter to respect the technique
you're using to prevent URL hyphenation by bracketing the URL with `nh`
and `hy` requests.  When the formatter reaches the end of an output line
and the hyphenation mode is nonzero _at that time_, it marches backwards
through the line and hyphenates accordingly.  This is a known defect of
both GNU and AT&T troffs.[2]  Worse, because the `hy` request without
arguments sets the hyphenation mode to "1", this approach defeats the
preferences of any groff users specifying `-rHY=0`, a feature supported
since groff 1.19 (April 2003).

[1] https://github.com/ThomasDickey/ncurses-snapshots/blob/4311b18dda3e30eed79bb7dca58cb52bab70c942/man/curs_getch.3x#L46
