@@@181a8533b23e8b5cb10a2d7bf4b3cb30cff8f99a	Use paragraphing macros instead of blank lines.	...to vertically separate text in man pages.

groff_man_style(7) offers advice about blank input lines.

     •  The empty request (.), which does nothing, vertically spaces the
        input file for readability by the document maintainer.  Do not
        put blank (empty) lines in a man page source document.  Some
        man(1) programs “squeeze” multiple blank output lines into one.

Also, when typesetting, the inter-paragraph distance is 0.4 vees
(except, in groff, when formatting an `EX`/`EE` example display), not
one vee.  A paragraphing macro puts the smaller vertical spacing on the
output, whereas the `sp` request without an argument puts one vee of
space on the output.

Using paragraphing macros makes the document look better when typeset.

Fixes:
$ nroff -rCHECKSTYLE=3 -ww -t -man -z *.nro 2>&1 | grep blank
an.tmac:less.nro:757: style: blank line in input
an.tmac:less.nro:850: style: blank line in input
an.tmac:less.nro:1437: style: blank line in input

less.nro.VER

@@@a54a9e17e99689e3899a6d3264e139290af4e9a3	less.nro.VER: Reformat prompt option material.	Set the various options as tagged paragraphs, like other options.

Use a relative inset (`RS`/`RE`) to get them inboard of the indented
paragraph in the context.

Prepend this list of options with a paragraphing macro to get vertical
space and improve the list's appearance (in my opinion).

groff man(7) discourages the use of leading spaces when filling is
enabled.

Fixes:
$ nroff -rCHECKSTYLE=3 -ww -t -man -z *.nro
an.tmac:less.nro:1110: style: 1 leading space(s) on input line
an.tmac:less.nro:1112: style: 1 leading space(s) on input line
an.tmac:less.nro:1113: style: 1 leading space(s) on input line
an.tmac:less.nro:1114: style: 1 leading space(s) on input line
an.tmac:less.nro:1115: style: 1 leading space(s) on input line
an.tmac:less.nro:1116: style: 1 leading space(s) on input line

less.nro.VER

@@@8f720a4281915196603911c8ab600e1047d421b3	Bracket example displays with `EX`/`EE` calls.	* less.nro.VER: Do it.
* lesskey.nro.VER: Do it.

These macros have a longer history than many appreciate.

groff_man_style(7):
     .EX
     .EE    Begin and end example.  After EX, filling is disabled (and,
            on typesetters, a monospaced font family is selected).
            Calling EE enables filling (and restores the previous
            family).

            Example regions are useful for formatting code, shell
            sessions, and text file contents.  An example region is not
            a “literal mode” of any sort: special character escape
            sequences must still be used to produce correct glyphs for
            ', -, \, ^, `, and ~ (see subsection “Portability” below).
            Sentence endings are still detected and supplemental inter‐
            sentence space applied.  If the amount of supplemental
            inter‐sentence spacing is altered, the rendering of, for
            instance, regular expressions using . or ? followed by
            multiple spaces can change.  Use the dummy character escape
            sequence \& before the spaces.

            Ninth Edition Unix introduced the EX and EE extensions.
            Documenter’s Workbench (DWB), Heirloom Doctools, and Plan 9
            troffs, and mandoc (since 1.12.2) support them.  Solaris 10
            troff does not.
...
   History
...
     ... Ninth Edition Unix (1986) introduced EX and EE.  ...

     Except for EX/EE, James Clark implemented the foregoing features in
     early versions of groff.  Later, groff 1.20 (2009) resurrected
     EX/EE and originated SY/YS, TQ, MT/ME, and UR/UE.

Add them because they are helpful to get groff and compatible formatters
to render these man pages, especially when typesetting, and do no harm
on formatters that don't, because we retain the (otherwise redundant)
`nf` and `fi` requests.

less.nro.VER
lesskey.nro.VER

@@@79a079ad6647ff2b6334da9584bdd70ed97a767b	Favor paragraphing macro calls over `sp` requests.	* less.nro.VER:
* lesskey.nro.VER: Do it.

groff_man_style(7):
     Several macros insert vertical space: SH, SS, TP, P (and its
     synonyms), IP, and HP.  They then enable no‐space mode; see
     groff(7).  The default inter‐section and inter‐paragraph spacing is
     1v for terminals and 0.4v for typesetters.  “v” is a unit of
     vertical distance, where 1v is the distance between adjacent text
     baselines.  (The deprecated macro PD can change this vertical
     spacing, but we discourage its use.)  Between EX and EE calls, the
     inter‐paragraph spacing is 1v regardless of output device.

This change eliminates extraneous blank lines before the "PROMPTS",
"PRECEDENCE", and "LESS ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES" section headings.

